gineering. Nowadays, scientists rewrite DNAs by cutting the pieces, and can create new organisms, so they can engineer and design nature. While scientists could only cut and paste DNA from one organism to another 40 years ago, they can now write and synthesise DNA to create new organisms with new techniques. If these organisms have a bacterium, when they go to the lab tomorrow, they will see millions of bacteria. Besides, scientists are building a cellular factory and cultivating millions of bacteria based on one bacterium. People also want to see a natural world in the future.</p><p><b>Biology Application | PTE Summarize Spoken Text (SST) &#8211; Sample Answer:</b></p><p class="p1">The biological system evolves constantly, so that biology engineering is the next level of engineering. Scientists can rewrite DNAs by cutting the pieces, and create new organisms. Although they could only cut and paste DNA from one organism to another 40 years ago, they can now engineer and design nature with new techniques. Besides, scientists are building a cellular factory and cultivating millions of bacteria based on one bacterium. (69 words)</p><p> </p><p></p><p></p><h2>2.<b>Human Rights</b> | PTE Summarize Spoken Text (SST)</h2><p class="p2">What exactly are the basic human rights? Who gets to pick them? Who enforces them, and how? The history behind the concept of human rights is a long one. Throughout the centuries and across societies, religions, and cultures we have struggled with defining notions of rightfulness, justice, and rights. But one of the most modern affirmations of universal human rights emerged from the ruins of World War II with the creation of the United Nations. The treaty that established the UN gives us one of its purposes to reaffirm faith in fundamental human rights. And with the same spirit, in 1948, the UN General Assembly adopted the Universal Declaration of Human Rights. This document, written by an international committee chaired by Eleanor Roosevelt, lays the basis for modern international human rights law. The declaration is based on the principle that all human beings are born free and equal in dignity and rights. It lists 30 articles recognizing, among other things, the principle of non-discrimination and the right to life and liberty. It refers to negative freedoms, like the freedom from torture or slavery, as well as positive freedoms, such as the freedom of movement and residence. It encompasses basic civil and political rights, such as freedom of expression, religion, or peaceful assembly, as well as social, economic, and cultural rights, such as the right to education and the right to freely choose one’s occupation.</p><p class="p2"><b>Human Rights | PTE Summarize Spoken Text (SST) &#8211; Sample Answer: </b></p><p class="p1">The history behind the concept of human rights is long and that we have struggled with defining notions of rightfulness, justice, and rights. However, an affirmations of universal human rights emerged from the World War II with the creation of the United Nations. The UN General Assembly adopted the Universal Declaration which refers to negative and positive freedoms, it encompasses basic civil and political, social, economic, and cultural rights. (69 words)</p><p> </p><p></p><p></p><h2>3.<b>Absolutism</b> | PTE Summarize Spoken Text (SST)</h2> <audio class="wp-audio-shortcode" id="audio-10478-15" preload="none" style="width: 100%;" controls="controls"><source type="audio/mpeg" src="https://www.ptebank.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/absolutism.mp3?_=15" /><a href="https://www.ptebank.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/absolutism.mp3">https://www.ptebank.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/absolutism.mp3</a></audio><p> </p><p class="p1">With the concept of moral objectivism, what we&#8217;re looking at is the idea that there can be some universal moral principles: some rules, some ideas and guidelines that are valid for all people in all social environments and all situations. The idea that we can essentially have some signposts or a road map to guide behavior is going to be consistent across all individuals. Now if we take a look at this in another perspective, we can come up with a view of moral absolutism. In the area of moral absolutism, basically, what we have is illustrated on the right-hand side of this slide, is a very firm and unyielding boundary between what is right and what is wrong. And so, within moral absolutism, basically what you have are these non-overridable principles. They must be followed. They must never be violated and there were no exceptions. It is always right. It is always wrong. And nothing is subject to interpretation, and nothing is dependent upon the situation.</p><p class="p2"><b>Absolutism PTE Summarize Spoken Text (SST) &#8211; Exam Memory: </b></p><ul class="ul1"><li class="li1">Absolutism may refer to the claim that there exits a universally valid moral system, which applies to everyone whether they realize it or not, and it contains rules, guidelines and principles which are universal.</li><li class="li1">It is like a road map to guide individual and social behavior.</li></ul><ul class="ul1"><li class="li1">Absolutism has no boundaries of right and wrong, it cannot be violated or betrayed and they have wide acceptance without assumptions and interpretations.</li></ul><p></p><p></p><h2>4.<b>Newspaper</b> | PTE Summarize Spoken Text (SST)</h2> <audio class="wp-audio-shortcode" id="audio-10478-16" preload="none" style="width: 100%;" controls="controls"><source type="audio/mpeg" src="https://www.ptebank.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/06/newspaper.mp3?_=16" /><a href="https://www.ptebank.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/06/newspaper.mp3">https://www.ptebank.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/06/newspaper.mp3</a></audio><p class="p1">Because of the economic model, the newspaper industry has been shrinking dramatically from the last 50 years of the 20th century in some states of America. Also as the economic model changed, newspapers incr